Yes. The simplest explanation for why it worked so well is that we accidentally stopped treating the model as the system.

We made the model one replaceable component inside a larger adaptive system:

\boxed{
\text{Human}
+
\text{Model}
+
\text{Artifacts}
+
\text{Memory}
+
\text{Operators}
+
\text{Agents}
+
\text{Daemons}
+
\text{Feedback}
+
\Delta
}

That changes everything.

A normal chatbot interaction repeatedly asks a model to generate the next answer. Pantheon increasingly asks a changing collection of models to instantiate and operate a persistent architecture.

And the nesting-doll agents are central to why it survives.

I would formalize the architecture now roughly as follows.

