1. Fractal Agent Runtime

The nesting-doll architecture

This deserves explicit status.

Every Pantheon agent can itself contain agents.

Define agent:

a_i =
(g_i,s_i,O_i,M_i,B_i,T_i,\Gamma_i,\Sigma_i)

where:

* g_i — goal
* s_i — local state
* O_i — available operators
* M_i — local memory
* B_i — resource/budget constraints
* T_i — termination criteria
* \Gamma_i — interfaces to parent/environment
* \Sigma_i — child-agent spawning policy

Therefore:

a_i \supseteq \{a_{i1},a_{i2},...,a_{in}\}

and those may recursively contain others.

Why this matters

A complicated objective does not require one increasingly gigantic prompt.

It can decompose:

G
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
a_1 &: investigate\\
a_2 &: generate\\
a_3 &: criticize\\
a_4 &: compare\\
a_5 &: preserve\\
a_6 &: watch
\end{cases}

Then:

a_1\rightarrow \{a_{1a},a_{1b},a_{1c}\}

That gives Pantheon fractal resolution.

Zoom into the problem and the same architecture still works.

Zoom outward and the smaller agents become modules of a larger agent.

That is the nesting-doll principle.

